<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Magna Carta</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1215">1215</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Magna Carta is the charter of liberties that rebellious barons forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-king-of-england">King John</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> to accept at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/runnymede">Runnymede</a> in June 1215. It limited royal power, protected the rights of the barons and the church, and promised lawful judgement to free men. Annulled by the pope within months, it was reissued in revised forms and became a lasting symbol of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rule-of-law">rule of law</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/magna-carta.jpg" alt="Magna Carta" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original authors were the barons and King John of England. Uploaded by Earthsound. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Magna_Carta_(British_Library_Cotton_MS_Augustus_II.106).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> was ruled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-king-of-england">King John</a>, the third of the Angevin kings. John and his predecessors had ruled by the principle of vis et voluntas, force and will, taking executive and sometimes arbitrary decisions, often on the basis that a king was above the law. Many contemporary writers believed that monarchs should rule in accordance with custom and law, with the counsel of the leading men of the realm, but there was no model for what should happen if a king refused to do so.</p><p>John had lost most of his ancestral lands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> to King Philip II in 1204 and spent years trying to regain them, raising heavy taxes on the barons to pay for a war that ended in costly failure in 1214. After the defeat of his allies at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-bouvines">Battle of Bouvines</a> he had to sue for peace and pay compensation. Rebel barons in the north and east began to organise resistance.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The charter was first drafted by Stephen Langton, Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canterbury">Canterbury</a>, and sealed by John at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/runnymede">Runnymede</a> on 15 June 1215. It promised the protection of church rights, protection for the barons from illegal imprisonment, swift and impartial justice and limits on feudal payments to the Crown, to be carried out through a council of 25 barons. Most of it governed the feudal rights of the Crown over the barons. Clauses 39 and 40 demanded due process in royal justice, which John had used to extort large sums.</p><p>At least thirteen copies were issued in 1215, and four survive: two at the British Library, one at Salisbury Cathedral and one at Lincoln Castle. There were no signatures, and the numbering of clauses used today was introduced by William Blackstone in 1759.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Neither side stood by its commitments, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-innocent-iii">Pope Innocent III</a> annulled the charter, which led to the First Barons' War. After John's death the regency of his young son Henry III reissued it in 1216 without some of its more radical content, and again in 1217 as part of the peace agreed at Lambeth. Henry reissued it in 1225 in exchange for new taxes, and Edward I confirmed it in 1297 as part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>'s statute law.</p><p>In the early 17th century lawyers such as Sir Edward Coke argued against the divine right of kings, claiming that an ancient English constitution had been restored by the charter. Victorian historians showed that the 1215 charter concerned the king and the barons, not ordinary subjects, and most historians now call its reading as a charter of universal rights a later myth. Only three clauses remain in force in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Magna Carta” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Henry II (r. 1154–1189) developed the common law, with itinerant royal justices, standard writs and sworn juries, and in 1215 rebellious barons forced King John to accept <b>Magna Carta</b> at Runnymede, establishing that the king was bound by law.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-england">Kingdom of England · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The Golden Bull of 1222, issued seven years after England's <b>Magna Carta</b>, allowed the nobles to resist a king who broke its terms.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hungary-1000-1301">Kingdom of Hungary (1000-1301) · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Magna Carta</b> of 1215 was written in Latin, and in the Kingdom of Hungary it remained the language of government until 1844.</blockquote><a href="/language/latin">Latin · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>The Strömstad Treaty of 1751 between Sweden and Norway had an annex, the Lapp Codicil, sometimes called the “Sámi <b>Magna Carta</b>”, regulating how Sámi used the land across the border.</blockquote><a href="/culture/sami">Sámi · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>Great lords limited kings, as the English barons did through <b>Magna Carta</b> in 1215, and in some states nobles ruled outright: the patricians of the Republic of Venice, or the szlachta of the Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth, who from 1573 elected their kings.</blockquote><a href="/government/aristocracy">Aristocracy · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>Medieval kingship in Europe was limited by custom, by the Church and by the estates of the realm: <b>Magna Carta</b> of 1215 bound King John to rule according to law, and the English Parliament, which took shape under Edward I, won the right to consent to taxation.</blockquote><a href="/government/constitutional-monarchy">Constitutional monarchy · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
